Preload libproj + libgdal before the extensions that link them, so iOS resolves them.

flet-libgdal ships a shared libgdal.dylib for iOS, and from its build 4 that
dylib links the shared libproj.dylib rather than absorbing PROJ -- so gdal,
fiona, rasterio, pyogrio and pyproj all resolve ONE GDAL and ONE PROJ. That is
what gives the app a single driver registry and a single PROJ database search
path, and it is why no per-extension registration patch is needed.

Delivery is the remaining problem: serious_python relocates each extension
module into its own *.framework bundle, while the dylibs stay plain files in
site-packages/opt/lib, and nothing on the relocated extensions' rpath points at
them. dyld then fails the @rpath lookup at first import:

    ImportError: dlopen(...): Library not loaded: @rpath/libgdal.dylib

Loading them RTLD_GLOBAL in dependency order (libproj, then libgdal, which
depends on it) sidesteps the search: dyld binds each @rpath reference by
matching an already-loaded image's install-id. The "<name>.fwork" fallback
covers a serious_python that does relocate them, and every failure is swallowed,
because on Android and on desktop this block must be inert rather than fatal.

Proven shape -- recipes/pyarrow and recipes/pymupdf both ship it and are
verified on device, as is the `av` recipe on the unmerged pyav branch, whose 49
extensions preload seven FFmpeg dylibs the same way.

--- a/fiona/__init__.py
+++ b/fiona/__init__.py
@@ -19,6 +19,51 @@
 features. One opened in a writing mode provides a ``write`` method.
 
 """
+
+# mobile-forge (iOS): the extension modules below link libgdal, which itself links libproj, through @rpath,
+# and flet relocates each of them into its own *.framework bundle while the
+# dylibs ship as plain files in site-packages/opt/lib. Nothing on the
+# extensions' rpath resolves that, so the first import otherwise dies with
+# "Library not loaded: @rpath/lib....dylib". Preload them RTLD_GLOBAL first, in
+# dependency order: once an image is loaded, dyld satisfies each @rpath
+# reference by matching the already-loaded image's install-id. Where a flet
+# build DID relocate one into a framework it leaves a "<name>.fwork" text
+# marker holding the app-relative path, so follow that instead. No-op on
+# Android, where DT_NEEDED resolves the .so by basename out of jniLibs, and on
+# desktop, where there is no opt/lib to find.
+try:
+    import ctypes as _mf_ctypes
+    import os as _mf_os
+
+    _mf_optlib = _mf_os.path.join(
+        _mf_os.path.dirname(_mf_os.path.dirname(_mf_os.path.abspath(__file__))),
+        "opt",
+        "lib",
+    )
+    for _mf_base in ("libproj", "libgdal"):
+        _mf_dylib = _mf_os.path.join(_mf_optlib, _mf_base + ".dylib")
+        if _mf_os.path.exists(_mf_dylib):
+            try:
+                _mf_ctypes.CDLL(_mf_dylib, mode=_mf_ctypes.RTLD_GLOBAL)
+            except OSError:
+                pass
+            continue
+        _mf_fwork = _mf_os.path.join(_mf_optlib, _mf_base + ".fwork")
+        if not _mf_os.path.exists(_mf_fwork):
+            continue
+        _mf_rel = open(_mf_fwork).read().strip()
+        _mf_dir = _mf_optlib
+        for _ in range(12):
+            _mf_dir = _mf_os.path.dirname(_mf_dir)
+            _mf_candidate = _mf_os.path.join(_mf_dir, _mf_rel)
+            if _mf_os.path.exists(_mf_candidate):
+                try:
+                    _mf_ctypes.CDLL(_mf_candidate, mode=_mf_ctypes.RTLD_GLOBAL)
+                except OSError:
+                    pass
+                break
+except Exception:
+    pass
 
